<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>netstandard1.1;netstandard2.0;netstandard2.1;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <Description>Data Standardizer provides implementations of various internationally recognised standards in data processing, covering topics ranging from languages to currencies and geographical entities. With strongly-typed enumerations for each standard (where applicable) or other targeted data types, you can represent these elements in your code such that errors with invalid values are minimised.

Provides types supporting the reading and writing of Internet messages, including:
- RFC 5322 and RFC 6532: a streaming reader of the header fields of a message, with UTF-8 in field bodies
- RFC 2045 and RFC 2046: a streaming reader of the MIME entities of a message, with multipart bodies, embedded messages and transfer decoding
- RFC 5322, RFC 6532, RFC 2045 and RFC 2046: streaming writers of header fields and MIME entities, with folding, transfer encoding, multipart boundaries and byte-exact copying of what has not changed
- RFC 5322, RFC 6532 and RFC 6854: typed addresses, mailboxes, groups, message identifiers and keyword phrases, parsed from the modern and obsolete syntax and written in the modern form where the modern syntax can express them
- RFC 5322: typed date-times, parsed from the modern and obsolete syntax and written in the modern form, which keep an unknown UTC offset (-0000), a leap second and whether a day of the week was given
- RFC 2047: encoded words in display names, keyword phrases, comments and unstructured fields, decoded when read and written where text is not US-ASCII
- RFC 2045, RFC 2183, RFC 2231, RFC 2392 and RFC 3282: the MIME-Version, Content-Type, Content-Transfer-Encoding, Content-ID, Content-Description, Content-Disposition and Content-Language header fields, with cid: URIs
- RFC 5322 and RFC 6854: a message object model with typed accessors for the origination, destination, identification, informational, resent and trace fields, a reader and writer that keep unchanged fields byte for byte, and the conformance checks of RFC 5322 §3.6
- RFC 2045, RFC 2046, RFC 2387 and RFC 2392: a MIME entity tree with decoded content, text and HTML bodies, attachments, cid: lookup, a message builder, and a writer that rewrites only what has changed
- RFC 2557 and RFC 2387: MHTML files, with Content-Location, the root of a multipart/related entity, URLs resolved to their parts, a document builder, and a reader and writer
- RFC 4155: mbox files in the mboxrd, mboxo, mboxcl and mboxcl2 variants, with a reader that takes one message at a time, so a mailbox of any size can be processed, and a writer
- Apple Mail EMLX files: a message with its byte count and its property list of metadata, with typed flags and dates, unknown keys kept, partial files with their parts supplied by the caller, and a reader and writer</Description>
    <Copyright>Copyright © 2026, Matthew25187. All rights reserved.</Copyright>
    <PackageTags>data;standard;email;internet message;file;rfc 5322;rfc 6532;rfc 6854;mime;rfc 2045;rfc 2046;rfc 2047;rfc 2183;rfc 2231;rfc 2387;rfc 2392;rfc 2557;rfc 3282;mhtml;rfc 4155;mbox;emlx;apple mail</PackageTags>
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard1.1'">
    <PackageReference Include="System.Net.Http" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DataStandardizer.Core\DataStandardizer.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DataStandardizer.Communication\DataStandardizer.Communication.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
